#import "void-import.typ": *

== Derived category statements

In this section, we translate the results developed through this chapter into derived category statements. We will translate the vanishing theorems first, since they give a valuable boundedness condition for translating proper base change. 

For the vanishing theorems, namely Artin vanishing (@artin-vanishing), Grothendieck vanishing (@grothendieck-vanishing) and their relative versions (@relative-artin-grothendieck-vanishing), we translate as below: 

#theorem(name: <derived-artin-grothendieck-vanishing>)[
  + (Absolute vanishing) Let $K$ be a field and $X$ be a finite type $K$-scheme. Suppose that $cd(K) < +oo$. 

    + (Artin) If $X$ is affine, then $R Gamma(X, -): D_#[tor] (X_et, Lambda) -> D(Lambda)$ has cohomological dimension above $<= cd(K) + dim(X)$; 

    + (Grothendieck) $R Gamma(X, -): D_#[tor] (X_et, Lambda) -> D(Lambda)$ has cohomological dimension above $<= cd(K) + 2 dim(X)$. 

    In both cases, $R Gamma(X, -)$ is bounded above. 

  + (Relative vanishing) Let $f: X -> Y$ be a morphism between schemes. 

    + (Artin) If $f$ is affine between finite type $K$-schemes for a field $K$, then $R f_*: D_#[tor] (X_et, Lambda) -> D(Y_et, Lambda)$ has cohomological dimension above $<= dim(X)$; 

    + (Grothendieck) If $f$ is between finite type $K$-schemes for a field $K$, then $R f_*: D_#[tor] (X_et, Lambda) -> D(Y_et, Lambda)$ has cohomological dimension above $<= 2 dim(X)$; 

    + (Fiberwise) If $f$ is proper with fibers having dimensions $<=d$, then $R f_*: D_#[tor] (X_et, Lambda) -> D(Y_et, Lambda)$ has cohomological dimension above $<=2d$. 

    In all of these cases, $R f_*$ is bounded above. 
]

For proper base change (@proper-base-change): 

#theorem(name: <derived-base-changes>)[
  In a Cartesian diagram: 
  // https://q.uiver.app/#r=typst&q=WzAsNCxbMCwwLCJYJyJdLFsxLDAsIlknIl0sWzAsMSwiWCJdLFsxLDEsIlkiXSxbMiwzLCJmIl0sWzAsMSwiZiciXSxbMSwzLCJnIl0sWzAsMiwiZyciLDJdXQ==
  #align(center, diagram({
    node((-1, -1), [$X'$])
    node((0, -1), [$Y'$])
    node((-1, 0), [$X$])
    node((0, 0), [$Y$])
    node((-0.7, -0.7), $cart$)
    edge((-1, 0), (0, 0), [$f$], label-side: left, "->")
    edge((-1, -1), (0, -1), [$f'$], label-side: left, "->")
    edge((0, -1), (0, 0), [$g$], label-side: left, "->")
    edge((-1, -1), (-1, 0), [$g'$], label-side: right, "->")
  }))
  Given $cal(K) in D(X_et, Lambda)$, the base change map: 
  $
    g^(-1) R f_* cal(K) -> R f'_* g'^(-1) cal(K)
  $
  is a quasi-isomorphism if one of the following holds: 

  + $f$ is finite;

  + $g$ is etale;

  + (Proper base change) $f$ is proper, $Y,Y'$ are locally noetherian, $cal(K) in D_#[tor] (X_et, Lambda)$; 
]
#proof[
  (1) can be checked termwise, and (2) can be checked by picking a K-injective resolution. 
  
  (3) $R f_*$ and $g^(-1)$ commute with arbitrary etale base change, so we may check Zariski locally on $Y$. Thus, we may assume that $Y = Spec(A)$ is affine. Now $X$ is proper over $Y$, hence at least of finite type. Thus $X$ is covered by finitely many finite type $A$-algebras. Suppose that all of them are generated by $d$ elements. Then the dimensions of all fibers of $X -> Y$ are $<=d$. Thus, by fiberwise Grothendieck vanishing, $R f_*: D_#[tor] (X_et, Lambda) -> D(Y_et, Lambda)$ is bounded above. Thus, both sides are bounded above and below, and the way-out lemma gives the proof for unbounded complexes. 
]
